		id: 'plain-meaning',
		heading: 'The plain meaning of “begotten”',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Begotten</em> is the old past participle of the verb <em>beget</em>, meaning to father a child. In sixteenth- and seventeenth-century English it was ordinary speech: a man begot sons and daughters. It has since dropped out of everyday use, surviving almost entirely inside older Bible translations and the creeds &mdash; which is why most people meet the word for the first time in John 3:16 and have to stop and ask what it means.</p><p>In the King James Version it renders the Greek word <em>monogenēs</em>, used of Jesus in five places in John&rsquo;s writings. So when a familiar Bible calls Jesus the &ldquo;only begotten Son,&rdquo; it is not describing a moment when the Son started existing. It is saying something about relationship: this Son is the Father&rsquo;s own, uniquely and irreplaceably.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'monogenes',
		heading: 'What the Greek word actually emphasises',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Monogenēs</em> is built from <em>monos</em> (only) and a root carrying the sense of kind or origin. Most modern scholarship reads it as <strong>&ldquo;one of a kind&rdquo;</strong> or <strong>&ldquo;only,&rdquo;</strong> stressing uniqueness rather than the mechanics of being born. The same word is used in Luke 7:12 of a widow&rsquo;s only son and in Hebrews 11:17 of Isaac &mdash; who was not Abraham&rsquo;s only child, but was his unique son of promise.</p><p>That is why nearly every modern translation moved away from &ldquo;only begotten.&rdquo; The older phrase invited a misunderstanding it never meant: that the Son was made or originated at some point. Historic Christian teaching has always said the opposite &mdash; the Son is eternally the Father&rsquo;s, &ldquo;begotten, not made,&rdquo; in the language of the Nicene Creed.</p><p>The translator&rsquo;s problem, then, is that the accurate old word has become an inaccurate signal to a modern ear.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'fhb-choice',
		heading: 'What the Father’s Heart Bible does with it',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible does not use the phrase &ldquo;only begotten&rdquo; anywhere. It renders <em>monogenēs</em> as <strong>&ldquo;his only Son&rdquo;</strong> or <strong>&ldquo;the one and only Son,&rdquo;</strong> keeping the uniqueness the Greek is pressing and dropping a word that now obscures more than it reveals.</p><p>There is one deliberate exception. In Psalm 2:7 the word is kept, because there the point is precisely the Father&rsquo;s eternal declaration over His Son: &ldquo;I will proclaim the decree of our Father: he said to me, &lsquo;You are my Son &mdash; Jesus &mdash; today I have begotten you.&rsquo;&rdquo; The New Testament quotes that verse of Jesus in Acts 13:33 and Hebrews 1:5 and 5:5, and the footnote in the text says so.</p><p>So the pattern is consistent rather than mechanical: plain English where &ldquo;begotten&rdquo; would confuse, and the older word retained in the one place where the Father-Son declaration is the whole subject.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'why-it-matters',
		heading: 'Why the choice matters for what you hear',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Read the two versions of John 3:16 side by side and the difference is not decorative. &ldquo;For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son&rdquo; puts two pieces of unfamiliar vocabulary between the reader and the sentence &mdash; a category word for the One who loves, and an archaic word for the One given.</p><p>The Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible reads: &ldquo;For our Father so loved the world that he gave his only Son.&rdquo; The relationship is now audible in both halves. A Father gave His own Son. That is what the verse has always said; the wording simply stops muffling it.</p><p>None of this is a claim that older translations are wrong. &ldquo;Only begotten&rdquo; was good English once. Language moved, and a translation made now has to serve the ear that is actually reading.</p>`,
	},
];

